{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And the waters which you shall take from the river shall become blood on the dry ground</b>; the word \"וְהָיוּ\" (\"and they shall be\") appearing twice — it appears to me that if it had said \"וְהָיוּ הַמַּיִם אֲשֶׁר תִּקַּח מִן הַיְאֹר לְדָם בַּיַּבָּשֶׁת,\" I would have understood that they turn into blood in his hand, and even when they are on the ground they would remain as they are. Now, however, the latter \"וְהָיוּ\" teaches us that they would not become blood until they were on the dry ground — this is the language of Rabbi Shlomo [Rashi]. But this meaning is not correct according to the Rav [Maimonides]. And there is no need for this midrashic interpretation, for the masters of the language [Radak in Sefer ha-Mikhlol, p. 61a] have already found that it is the way of many verses to repeat words for emphasis and reinforcement, or because of a long phrase that intervenes between them. And similar to it: \"And your valuation shall be from twenty years old even to sixty years old, and your valuation shall be, for the male, fifty shekels of silver\" (Leviticus 27:3). And likewise: \"And when the Levite comes from one of your gates, from all Israel where he sojourns, and comes with all the desire of his soul\" (Deuteronomy 18:6) — he repeated \"and comes\" a second time because he lengthened the phrase between them. And likewise: \"And the king of Egypt spoke to the Hebrew midwives, of whom the name of the one was Shifrah and the name of the other Puah; and he said, When you deliver…\" etc. (above, 1:15–16). \"And Elohim spoke to Israel in visions of the night, and said, Jacob, Jacob\" (Genesis 46:2). And of this nature there are many."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:02:32.169656570Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}